  "body": "From the review of PR #204 (https://github.com/NeoTech/Camcraft/pull/204#issuecomment-6714604132). These reproduce on **main**, without #204.\n\n- A plate with a turned boss r5 at (10, 0) reads a **trusted** axis of (0.707, 0, 0.707), at 45°.\n- A long shaft r10 h150, meshed with one band along its length, with a D-flat and a cross hole along X, reads X instead of Z (trusted, 4.2% set aside).\n\nA trusted wrong axis puts every turned and cross-drilled feature about the wrong line: a gouge.\n\nRoot cause: the full-facet concurrency test asks whether each facet's normal line meets the candidate axis within tolerance. Two-triangle faces and single-band walls have normal lines that all pass through ONE point (the face or part centre), so they 'meet' any line through that point. The test can't tell point-concurrency from axis-concurrency.\n\nDone means: the fit requires real axis concurrency. For example, the inliers' normal lines must meet the axis at points spread along it (axial coverage of the contact points), or the facets must be sub-sampled so one big face counts by its area along the axis. Alternatively, a part whose inliers are dominated by point-concurrent faces is refused by name. Pins: both fixtures above refused (or read correctly), and every existing turned-bar, short-bar and keyed-shaft pin still correct.",
